{
  "summary": "This study investigates how dual twisted interfaces in CVD-grown trilayer MoS2 affect second harmonic generation (SHG). By fabricating nine distinct stacking configurations, researchers analyzed Raman and photoluminescence data to identify stacking-dependent phonon and excitonic responses. Atomic-resolution techniques confirmed dual twisted interfaces and a supermoiré lattice. The study developed a framework linking interface geometry to SHG intensity and polarization, demonstrating TT-MoS2's potential for tunable nonlinear optical engineering.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Dual twisted interfaces in trilayer MoS2 create stacking-dependent phonon and excitonic responses",
    "Atomic-resolution techniques confirmed dual twisted interfaces and a supermoiré lattice structure",
    "A framework linking interface geometry to SHG intensity and polarization was established"
  ],
  "study_type": "CVD-grown twisted trilayer MoS2 configurations",
  "scientific_significance": "This work provides a foundational understanding of multi-interface twisted coupling in van der Waals materials, enabling precise engineering of nonlinear optical responses through structural design."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates how spontaneous phase separation in Janus hydrogels spatiotemporally orchestrates fibrotic progression for functional tendon regeneration. The researchers engineered a Janus hydrogel (SPS-J. The hydrogel's outer hyaluronic acid layer reduces adhesions, while the inner PLD layer provides mechanical strength. It temporally regulates fibroblast activity and macrophage polarization via MMP-2-degradable siRNA and niclosamide release, promoting functional tendon repair in rats.",
  "key_findings": [
    "SPS-JH hydrogel reduces peritendinous adhesions in rat Achilles tendon injuries",
    "The hydrogel's outer hyaluronic acid layer establishes a lubricated anti-adhesion interface",
    "The hydrogel's inner PLD layer sustains niclosamide release to suppress fibrogenesis during proliferation"
  ],
  "study_type": "In vitro and in vivo rat model",
  "scientific_significance": "This work demonstrates that spontaneous phase separation can coordinate fibrotic progression dynamics to enable functional tendon regeneration through precise spatiotemporal control of cellular responses."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates the mechanism behind sex-based differences in chronic liver disease (CLD) using metabolomics data from 9,254 individuals. It identifies isoursodeoxycholic acid (isoUDCA) as a bile acid mediator of female hepatoprotection, originating from a bacterial consortium enriched by estrogen. The findings suggest a microbiome-bile acid-liver axis that could explain sex disparities in CL. The study implies isoUDCA's potential as a therapeutic target.",
  "key_findings": [
    "IsoUDCA, an endogenous bile acid, mediates female hepatoprotection in chronic liver disease.",
    "Estrogen selectively enriches a bacterial consortium that produces isoUDCA.",
    "IsoUDCA promotes SREBP2 nuclear accumulation, enhancing cholesterol handling via STARD4 and CYP7B1."
  ],
  "study_type": "Metabolomics analysis of large-scale human data",
  "scientific_significance": "The study establishes a microbiome-bile acid axis that explains sex-based differences in chronic liver disease susceptibility, providing a mechanistic basis for potential sex-specific therapeutic strategies."
}

{
  "summary": "This study addresses the challenge of achieving low threshold voltage (Vth) and high performance in n-type organic electrochemical transistors (OECTs). Using a stepwise cyanation strategy on quinoxaline-based polymers, researchers developed QxCN-CNTVT with a deep LUMO level (-4.23 eV). The material enabled OECTs with a low Vth of 0.13 V, high µC* value (168.89 F cm-1 V-1 s-1), and stable operation. The device demonstrated practical applications in amplifying electrooculogram and electrocardiogram signals and implementing logic gates.",
  "key_findings": [
    "QxCN-CNTVT exhibits a deep LUMO level of -4.23 eV",
    "OECTs achieve a low threshold voltage of 0.13 V and high µC* value of 168.89 F cm-1 V-1 s-1",
    "The material enables stable operation and practical applications in EOG/ECG signal amplification and logic gates"
  ],
  "study_type": "In vitro synthesis and device testing",
  "scientific_significance": "This work provides a molecular design strategy to overcome the performance-threshold voltage trade-off in n-type OECTs, advancing low-power bioelectronic applications."
}

{
  "summary": "This study addresses residual tumor persistence after high-frequency irreversible electroporation (H-FIRE) for prostate cancer. By identifying DNA damage repair as the key mechanism for reversible cell survival, researchers engineered piezoelectric nanomaterial BOIS. H-FIRE-enhanced membrane permeability allows BOIS delivery to residual cells. Ultrasound activation triggers BOIS to generate electric fields and reactive oxygen species, causing irreversible cell death through dual mechanisms. This approach significantly improves tumor eradication efficacy.",
  "key_findings": [
    "H-FIRE-induced membrane permeability enables efficient BOIS delivery to residual prostate cancer cells",
    "Ultrasound-activated BOIS produces electric fields and reactive oxygen species to induce irreversible cell death",
    "The dual mechanism converts reversibly injured residual cells into irreversible lethal damage"
  ],
  "study_type": "",
  "scientific_significance": "This approach establishes a self-reinforcing therapeutic loop where focal ablation delivers nanomaterials to enhance residual tumor elimination, offering a novel strategy for improving focal therapy outcomes."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates how lifestyle factors influence immune checkpoint blockade toxicity by developing the LISA model. It demonstrates diet-induced metabolic and microbial changes cause regulatory T cell fragility, leading to multi-organ immune-related adverse events. Key factors include sex, age, and immunotherapy response, with interventions like dietary reversal and IFN-γ blockade resolving inflammation.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Diet-driven metabolic and microbial alterations induce regulatory T cell fragility",
    "LISA model recapitulates multi-organ immune-related adverse events with immune infiltration and cytokine production",
    "Dietary reversal and IFN-γ blockade restore regulatory T cell stability and resolve inflammation"
  ],
  "study_type": "Lifestyle-induced susceptibility to irAEs (Lusing the LISA model)",
  "scientific_significance": "This work identifies dietary, microbial, and immune mechanisms underlying immune-related adverse events during immunotherapy, providing a preclinical model to mitigate toxicity while enhancing treatment efficacy."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates closed nanopores in silicon-carbon anodes as stress-regulation units to mitigate lithiation-induced deformation and interphase degradation. By engineering heteroatom-doped carbon hosts with abundant closed pores, the material redirects expansion stress inward, suppresses electrode swelling, and improves Li+ kinetics. The nitrogen-doped composite achieves 1756.5 mAh g-1 reversible capacity, 90.2% initial Coulombic efficiency, and 868.4 mAh g-1 at 3 C, with reduced expansion from 38.4% to 13.1% after 50 cycles. The design enables scalable production and high cycling stability in pouch cells.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Closed nanopores in heteroatom-doped carbon hosts redirect expansion stress inward, suppressing electrode swelling",
    "Nitrogen-doped silicon-carbon composites achieve 1756.5 mAh g-1 reversible capacity and 868.4 mAh g-1 at 3 C",
    "The design reduces electrode thickness expansion from 38.4% to 13.1% after 50 cycles and enables 80% capacity retention in pouch cells after 1200 cycles"
  ],
  "study_type": "Material design and synthesis",
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes closed-pore stress regulation as a scalable structural strategy for practical silicon-carbon anodes, addressing mechanical instability while maintaining electrochemical performance."
}

{
  "summary": "This pilot study evaluated woven allogeneic vascular grafts made from cell-assembled extracellular matrix (CAM) as arteriovenous shunts in sheep. The biological tissue-engineered grafts demonstrated superior patency (100% vs. 87.5% for ePTFE) and reduced thrombosis risk compared to synthetic controls. Histological analysis showed early neotissue formation and endothelialization without significant complications at 8 weeks.",
  "key_findings": [
    "100% patency of CAM grafts versus 87.5% for ePTFE controls at 4 weeks",
    "Histological evidence of neotissue formation with smooth muscle cells and endothelialization by 8 weeks",
    "Localized calcification correlated with high tissue compaction in CAM grafts"
  ],
  "study_type": "Pilot study in sheep",
  "scientific_significance": "This work demonstrates the feasibility of biological tissue-engineered vascular grafts using woven cell-assembled extracellular matrix for hemodialysis access, offering a potential alternative to synthetic materials with improved biocompatibility and reduced thrombosis risk."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigated metabolic heterogeneity in small cell lung cancer (SCLC) by analyzing transcriptomic and metabolomic data across neuroendocrine (NE) and non-NE cellular states. It identified a universal dependency on exogenous cysteine/cystine (Cys) and demonstrated that NE cells with low ASCL1 levels undergo ferroptosis upon Cys depletion, while ASCL1high cells undergo apoptosis and resist ferroptosis due to GCH1-mediated BH4/BH2 upregulation. Combined Cys depletion and BH4/BH2 inhibition reduced tumor growth in patient-derived xenografts.",
  "key_findings": [
    "SCLC cells exhibit universal dependency on exogenous cysteine/cystine across neuroendocrine and non-neuroendocrine states",
    "ASCL1low neuroendocrine SCLC cells undergo ferroptosis when cysteine is depleted",
    "ASCL1high SCLC cells resist ferroptosis via GCH1-driven BH4/BH2 upregulation, enabling apoptosis"
  ],
  "study_type": "Integrated transcriptomic and metabolomic analyses",
  "scientific_significance": "The findings establish metabolic state as a determinant of cell death susceptibility in SCLC, providing a mechanistic basis for targeting cysteine metabolism to overcome therapy resistance."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates programmable structural color lifetimes in polymer systems for dynamic optical encryption. By engineering activation energy through crosslinking density and amplitude control, the research encodes spatiotemporal evolution of structural color. The approach enables independent programming of color lifetime across regions, spanning 300 seconds to over 240 days via segmental relaxation kinetics, with applications in secure optical encryption and cold-chain monitoring.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Programmable structural color lifetime spans 300 seconds to over 240 days",
    "Activation energy engineering via crosslinking density and amplitude control enables spatiotemporal encoding",
    "Segmental relaxation kinetics drive amplitude decay, redistributing diffracted energy to cause structural color fading"
  ],
  "study_type": "Dynamic surface grating with photomask-assisted UV exposure",
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes activation energy as a fundamental handle for temporal programmability in dynamic optical systems, advancing structural color engineering for high-security applications."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates radiation-induced brain injury (RIBI) by targeting neuronal ferroptosis, cuproptosis, and apoptosis. Using a nose-to-brain delivery nanodot (SPDA-SeMet), the researchers restored metal-redox homeostasis and mitochondrial integrity, significantly outperforming existing therapies. The nanoplatform simultaneously inhibits all three death pathways through enhanced metal chelation and ROS scavenging.",
  "key_findings": [
    "SPDA-SeMet nanodot effectively inhibits neuronal apoptosis, ferroptosis, and cuproptosis in RIBI",
    "Nose-to-brain delivery via ultrasmall size and negative charge enables rapid mucus penetration",
    "Restoration of ROS/Fe3+/Cu2+ homeostasis preserves mitochondrial integrity and reduces inflammation"
  ],
  "study_type": "In vivo mouse model",
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes a multimodal nanoregulator strategy to simultaneously target multiple cell death pathways in radiation-induced brain injury, advancing therapeutic approaches for complex neurodegenerative processes."
}

{
  "summary": "This study used multimodal spatial transcriptomics to investigate the relationship between CAG repeat expansions, HTT inclusions, and neuronal loss in Huntington's disease. Analysis of 428,1.73 cells revealed layer 5-6 near-projecting neurons and L6b deep-layer excitatory neurons show >50% loss linked to large somatic repeats (>450 CAGs). Intranuclear inclusions peaked at intermediate repeats (250-350 CAGs) and correlated with broader transcriptional changes.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Layer 5-6 near-projecting neurons and L6b deep-layer excitatory neurons undergo >50% loss in Huntington's disease cortex",
    "Large somatic CAG expansions (>450 CAGs) correlate with selective neuronal loss in HD",
    "Intranuclear inclusions are most prevalent at intermediate somatic repeat expansions (250-350 CAGs)"
  ],
  "study_type": "Multimodal spatial transcriptomics",
  "scientific_significance": "The study establishes a direct link between somatic CAG repeat expansions, HTT inclusions, and selective neuronal vulnerability in Huntington's disease, providing a framework for understanding pathogenic mechanisms across cell types."
}

{
  "summary": "This review examines minimally invasive in vivo fabrication of multiscale biohybrid systems for diagnosis, monitoring, and tissue regeneration. It identifies three key principles: multiscale coupling bridges material architecture with biological responses; spatiotemp: coordination integrates precise in vivo construction with dynamic evolution; and sensing-regeneration feedback enables responsive therapeutic regulation. The study highlights machine learning-assisted design and multi-site fabrication as critical pathways toward adaptive, closed-loop healthcare systems.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Multiscale coupling bridges material architecture with cellular and tissue responses",
    "Spatiotemporal coordination integrates precise in vivo construction with dynamic material and biological evolution",
    "Sensing-regeneration feedback extends biohybrid systems from passive repair to responsive therapeutic regulation"
  ],
  "study_type": "",
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes foundational principles for designing minimally invasive multiscale biohybrid systems that can dynamically interface with biological processes, advancing the field toward responsive, closed-loop therapeutic applications."
}

{
  "summary": "This study developed an ultrasound-programmable poly(amino acid) nanocomposite to synchronize intracellular release of Fe(II) and cisplatin in multidrug-resistant cancer cells. The nanocomposite triggered reactive oxygen species overproduction and mitochondrial damage, enhancing autophagy. In vitro and in vivo results demonstrated significant tumor growth inhibition and prolonged survival in MDR lung cancer models.",
  "key_findings": [
    "EG-KFc20-KSA/CDDP nanocomposite enabled synchronized release of Fe(II) and cisplatin in tumor microenvironments",
    "Treatment elevated intracellular ROS by 2.7-fold and reduced glutathione to 31.2% of control levels",
    "The strategy achieved 85.3% tumor inhibition and extended survival to 61 days in MDR lung cancer mice"
  ],
  "study_type": "In vitro and in vivo studies",
  "scientific_significance": "This work provides a mechanistically defined approach to overcome multidrug resistance by synchronizing metal ion and chemotherapy delivery to amplify oxidative stress, offering a novel strategy for enhancing platinum-based cancer therapy efficacy."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates cMAF's role in regulating brain perivascular macrophages via insulin-like growth factor 1 (IGF1) production, critical for cerebrovascular function. The research demonstrates cMA:IGF1 signaling is heightened in Alzheimer's disease, suggesting a protective mechanism that diminishes in APOE4 carriers, potentially indicating a vulnerability in cerebrovascular health.",
  "key_findings": [
    "cMAF regulates brain perivascular macrophages through IGF1 production",
    "cMAF-IGF1 signaling is elevated in Alzheimer's disease",
    "The protective cMAF-IGF1 response is compromised in APOE4 carriers"
  ],
  "study_type": "",
  "scientific_significance": "The identification of cMAF as a regulator of cerebrovascular function via perivascular macrophages provides insight into potential protective mechanisms against cerebrovascular decline in neurodegenerative diseases."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates using pollen-encapsulated liquid marbles to create dual-porosity ceramic beads for environmental remediation. By UV irradiation and mechanical vibration, the researchers transitioned wetting behavior from Cassie-Baxter to Cassie-impregnating, enabling efficient pollutant capture. The resulting beads achieved up to .288 times higher removal efficiency for heavy metals and nanoplastics compared to conventional methods, with pollen species showing selective pollutant adsorption.",
  "key_findings": [
    "UV irradiation and mechanical vibration switch liquid marble wetting state from Cassie-Baxter to Cassie-impregnating",
    "Pollen-encapsulated ceramic beads achieve 2.88x higher pollutant removal efficiency for heavy metals and nanoplastics",
    "Different pollen species demonstrate distinct selectivity for target pollutants"
  ],
  "study_type": "One-step conversion process using UV irradiation and mechanical vibration",
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes a novel method for creating highly efficient, selective ceramic adsorbents using renewable pollen, advancing environmental remediation technologies through dual-porosity design and tunable pollutant capture."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates hydrogel adhesives designed for hemorrhage treatment and wound closure with phase-transition-regulated wet adhesion and removal. The researchers developed a hydrogel (PANA) that exhibits strong wet adhesion (61.2 ± 9 kPa) at body temperature and weak adhesion (5.3 ± 2.2 kPa) at 20 °C for easy detachment. The hydrogel uses N-acryloyl-L-phenylalanine to enable interfacial drainage, matrix cohesion, and thermo-triggered phase transitions. It achieves rapid hemostasis in rabbit injuries and allows gentle removal with cold plasma activation to prevent secondary damage and bacterial growth.",
  "key_findings": [
    "PANA hydrogel demonstrates strong wet adhesion (61.2 ± 9 kPa) at body temperature for effective hemorrhage control",
    "The hydrogel shows weak adhesion (5.3 ± 2.2 kPa) at 20 °C enabling gentle removal without secondary injury",
    "N-acryloyl-L-phenylalanine in the hydrogel network facilitates interfacial drainage, matrix cohesion, and thermo-triggered phase transitions"
  ],
  "study_type": "In vitro and in vivo testing in rabbit models",
  "scientific_significance": "This work provides a clinically viable solution for balancing robust wet adhesion with on-demand removal in hemorrhage management, addressing a critical limitation in current surgical adhesives."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates molecular mechanisms coordinating floral rewards and pollinator attraction in melons. By identifying nectarless1 (nls1), which encodes YABBY5 (YAB5), the research demonstrates YAB5's role in nectary development, starch remobilization for nectar, and UV nectar guide formation via flavonoid pathways. Disruption of these genes reduces pollinator visitation, revealing how a developmental regulator synchronizes reward and signal production.",
  "key_findings": [
    "YAB5 integrates nectary identity specification with starch remobilization for nectar secretion",
    "YAB5 activates DPE2 to promote starch remobilization during nectar production",
    "YAB5 regulates UV nectar guides through MYB111-dependent flavonoid pathways"
  ],
  "study_type": "Genetic analysis of melon diversity",
  "scientific_significance": "This work elucidates a conserved molecular mechanism where a single transcription factor coordinates floral organ development with metabolic and signaling pathways to attract pollinators, advancing understanding of plant-pollinator coevolution."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates how functional group transposition in dithienopyrrole-based self-assembled molecules enables switching between antiparallel and parallel molecular packing. By swapping ortho-/para- positions of trifluoromethyl and chlorine groups, the researchers achieved uniform hole transport layers on ITO with enhanced active layer crystallinity. Optimized organic solar cells reached 18.70% power conversion efficiency, demonstrating improved charge dynamics for high-performance optoelectronics.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Swapping ortho-/para- positions of trifluoromethyl and chlorine on phenyl termini switches molecular stacking from disordered antiparallel to well-ordered parallel",
    "Optimized organic solar cells achieved 18.70% power conversion efficiency with enhanced crystallinity and vertical phase segregation",
    "The SAMs showed versatility across binary/ternary systems, maintaining high efficiency even at scaled-up device areas"
  ],
  "study_type": "Self-assembled molecules (SAMs) with dithienopyrrole (DTP) core",
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes functional group transposition as a precise molecular strategy to co-engineer interfacial order and active-layer morphology, advancing high-efficiency organic photovoltaics and photodetectors."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates industrialization's impact on the human metaorganism by analyzing gut microbiome, immunoglobulin A, host genotyping, diet, lifestyle, and fecal biomarkers across globally diverse populations. It demonstrates industrialization correlates with homogenized microbial compositions, reduced diversity, lower community stability, elevated gut stress markers, increased IgA secretion, and altered IgA-bacteria interactions. Microbiome disease predictors trained in industrialized groups fail in less industrialized cohorts, indicating limited cross-population transferability of microbiome-based applications.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Industrialization correlates with homogenized microbial compositions",
    "Industrialization is linked to reduced microbial diversity and lower community stability",
    "Microbiome disease predictors trained in industrialized populations are uninformative in less industrialized cohorts"
  ],
  "study_type": "",
  "scientific_significance": "The findings reveal coordinated restructuring of the microbial ecosystem and host physiology due to industrialization, emphasizing the need for globally representative data to improve microbiome-based translational applications across diverse human populations."
}

{
  "summary": "This study addresses the challenge of integrating biological signal processing with hardware-level data security in bioelectronic systems. It introduces a conjugated radical polymer that demonstrates stochastic electronic behavior and bioelectronic signal transduction within a single material. The polymer exhibits physical unclonable function characteristics with uniqueness near 50% and enables highly sensitive dopamine detection at 10 pM.",
  "key_findings": [
    "The conjugated radical polymer shows stochastic resistive switching with physical unclusing function characteristics approaching 50% uniqueness",
    "The material enables highly sensitive dopamine detection with a detection limit of ∼10 pM",
    "Sensing response aligns with redox-mediated coupling between dopamine and the radical-containing backbone"
  ],
  "study_type": "Material-based platform",
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes a material-level strategy that links stochastic electronic functionality and bioelectronic sensing, advancing secure, adaptive, and biointegrated electronic systems."
}

{
  "summary": "This study analyzed 212 individuals across 7,000 years in the eastern Colombian Andes using genome-wide data to investigate subsistence transitions and population dynamics. It found that hunter-gatherer lineages persisted for five millennia before maize adoption around 2,800 BP, with genetic continuity persisting despite subsistence change. A population shift occurred ∼2,200 BP, linked to migration from admixed groups with Chibchan-Andean ancestry.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Hunter-gatherer lineages persisted for five millennia without external gene flow",
    "Two individuals from ∼2,800 BP show maize consumption while retaining autochthonous ancestry",
    "Herrera Period emergence coincided with genetic break by ∼2,000 BP, indicating migration from admixed Chibchan-Andean groups"
  ],
  "study_type": "Genome-wide analysis of archaeological samples",
  "scientific_significance": "The findings decouple subsistence transition from population replacement in the Andes, demonstrating long-term genetic continuity despite dietary shifts and providing insights into the timing and drivers of cultural change in prehistoric South America."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates supramolecular adhesives with tunable crystallinity for extreme environments. Using tannic acid and poly(ε-caprolact. The adhesive demonstrates exceptional strength (8.5 MPa at 25°C, 6.6 MPa at -196°C) and stability in corrosive underwater conditions and organic solvents, outperforming commercial hot-melt adhesives. The findings highlight crystallinity's critical role in adhesion performance under harsh conditions.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Adhesive achieves 8.5 MPa at 25°C and 6.6 MPa at -196°C",
    "Stability in corrosive underwater environments (acidic, alkaline, saline) and organic solvents",
    "Crystallinity modulation governs adhesion performance in extreme conditions"
  ],
  "study_type": "Theoretical and experimental investigations",
  "scientific_significance": "This work establishes a bioinspired design strategy for supramolecular adhesives that maintain performance across ultralow temperatures, corrosive environments, and organic solvents, advancing materials for extreme-condition applications."
}

{
  "summary": "This study used CRISPR tiling mutagenesis to map the functional landscape of the 10-kb MYC locus at base-pair resolution. It revealed 67% of essential base pairs are noncoding, with an ultraconserved RNA element in the 3' UTR promoting MYC mRNA perinuclear localization and protein nuclear localization via hnRNP L interaction. Steric-blocking ASOs targeting this element selectively eliminate MYC-dependent cancer cells and inhibit tumor growth in organoids.",
  "key_findings": [
    "67% of functionally essential base pairs in the MYC locus are noncoding",
    "An ultraconserved noncoding RNA element in the MYC 3' UTR regulates MYC mRNA and protein localization via hnRNP L",
    "Steric-blocking antisense oligonucleotides targeting this RNA element selectively kill MYC-dependent cancer cells and inhibit tumor growth in organoids"
  ],
  "study_type": "CRISPR tiling mutagenesis",
  "scientific_significance": "This work demonstrates that noncoding sequences carry major functional genetic information and identifies a druggable RNA element targeting MYC-driven cancer pathways."
}
